    "body": "### Description\n\nCurrently, PostgreSQL sink connectors are configured with a fixed schema pattern using:\n\n```json\n\"table.name.format\": \"<SERVICE>.${topic}\"\n```\n\nThis forces all tables for a given service to be created under a single fixed schema (`<SERVICE>`). This limitation prevents more flexible database layouts, such as having **multiple schemas per service**, for example one schema per vertical or logical domain within the same customer/service database.\n\nThe goal of this issue is to **study the feasibility and design options** to make the schema component **dynamic and configurable**, instead of being fixed to the FIWARE service.\n\n### Context\n\nThis topic is closely related to the existing SQL datamodels logic discussed in:\n\n* **implement SQL datamodels logic #18**\n* Cygnus SQL datamodels documentation:\n  [https://github.com/telefonicaid/fiware-cygnus/blob/master/doc/cygnus-ngsi/flume_extensions_catalogue/ngsi_postgresql_sink.md](https://github.com/telefonicaid/fiware-cygnus/blob/master/doc/cygnus-ngsi/flume_extensions_catalogue/ngsi_postgresql_sink.md)\n\nCurrently, only two datamodels are considered relevant:\n\n* `dm-by-entity-type-database` (current, already implemented)\n* `dm-by-fixed-entity-type-database-schema` (future)\n\nIn the current Kafnus-NGSI implementation:\n\n* The stream app builds the final SQL table name (using `fiware-service` and `entityType`)\n* This table name is sent via the `target_table` header\n* A custom SMT replaces the topic name with the final table name so the sink connector inserts directly into it\n\nAs discussed in comments on #18, a possible evolution would be:\n\n* Kafnus-NGSI sends **base metadata only** (e.g. `fiware-service`, `entityType`, maybe `servicePath` or vertical)\n* The **connector / SMT constructs the final schema + table name** according to a configured datamodel\n\nThis approach would allow:\n\n* Supporting multiple SQL datamodels without modifying NGSI logic\n* Making schema selection dynamic (e.g. per vertical)\n* Cleaner separation of responsibilities and better extensibility\n\n### Objectives of this Issue\n\n* Analyze limitations of the current fixed-schema approach\n* Explore options to make the schema name dynamic in PostgreSQL connectors\n* Evaluate whether this should be handled:\n\n  * via connector configuration\n  * via SMT logic\n  * or via a combination of both\n* Assess compatibility with existing datamodels and current deployments",
